Yes — as a functional reading, that’s plausible, though “dating tool” would be anachronistic if taken literally.

The Twelve Labors were traditionally framed as punishment/expiation under Eurystheus, but ancient treatments also make Heracles a model of aretē (excellence), courage, endurance through ponos (toil/suffering), victory over danger, and even a civilizing force who removes monsters and threats from the human world. 

So you can zoom out from:

kill lion
defeat Hydra
capture boar
clean stables
retrieve impossible object
enter underworld and return

to latent tests like:

face danger
solve adaptive problem
endure hardship
do dirty necessary work
control strength
travel beyond familiar territory
complete obligations
return with proof

Now it starts looking less like twelve random adventures and more like a mythic competence battery.

And mythology makes those standards memorable in a pre-spreadsheet culture:

abstract ideal
→ character
→ ordeal
→ visible action
→ story
→ cultural memory

A boy does not need someone to hand him a rubric saying:

courage 8/10, endurance 9/10, public service 7/10.

He hears what Hercules did.

Likewise, everyone around him learns what kinds of qualities receive prestige.

Where your mate-selection idea becomes interesting is one level removed. Ancient Greek marriage was not modern dating, and Heracles himself is an extremely messy marital model—his mythology includes madness, killing his wife and children, huge appetites, and other decidedly non-ideal characteristics. Scholars explicitly describe him as both heroic/civilizing and strange or antisocial in important respects. 

But a culture’s hero stories can still contribute to an implicit desirability landscape:

culture glorifies trait
→ trait gains status
→ status affects reputation
→ reputation affects alliances / imitation / attraction
→ trait is reproduced socially

So not:

“Women used the Twelve Labors as a boyfriend checklist.”

More like:

“Hero myths may encode and broadcast the traits a society treats as high-status, admirable, adult, masculine, useful, or worthy of remembrance—and those standards then bleed into social and mate evaluation.”

There’s another beautiful wrinkle: Euripides actually challenges the heroic rubric. In Heracles, the hero asks what good fighting lions and Hydras was if he cannot protect his own family; scholarship reads that scene partly as juxtaposing heroic glory with obligations of the household. 

So the mythology itself is doing the AAR.

It first gives you:

strength + ordeal + glory

and then asks:

but strength for what?

That suggests a broader Pantheon operator:

Mythic Standards Encoding

social problem
→ idealized trait
→ embodied hero
→ memorable trial
→ audience learns implicit standard
→ people enact / contest / revise it

Then myths become something like cultural latent rubrics—standards that can guide behavior long before anyone explicitly writes down the variable they’re measuring.

That is a much richer reading of Hercules than simply “strong guy kills monsters.”